            <ArticleTitle>Shoulder Arthroplasty: Historical Considerations.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1100-1107</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2174/1874325001711011100</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Background" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The first articular metal prosthesis was implanted in the shoulder more than 120 years ago. The aim of this paper is to report shoulder arthroplasty evolution during this time thru the literature of the twentieth century.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">A literature review was performed selecting the founding papers about shoulder arthroplasty.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">After being almost forgotten during the first part of the 20th century, various implants were introduced in the 1950s with Charles Neer as a leader. The reverse concept appeared in the 1970s and knew many failures before Grammont's design.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusion" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">After many unfortunate trials, the shoulder prosthesis is now widely disseminated with products of many companies.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
